<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>An example of the magical spell scripting DSL (left) and the cellular automata DSL (right). In natural language, these are “A controllable wind pixie that warps me when I call,” and “a cloudy gas that diffuses randomly.”</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experiment 2: Bidirectional Translation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment evaluated how well a user’s creative intent is preserved in a “round-trip” translation, simulating the process of a player articulating a mental concept. Using the bidirectional set, an LLM had to recreate an original, procedurally-generated DSL script using only its corresponding natural language description. Description length and style were compared in a 2x2 factorial design. We assessed the difference between the original and final scripts using two algorithmic similarity measures: (1) tree edit distance, computed between the Abstract Syntax Trees (ASTs) of the scripts using the APTED algorithm to quantify structural changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pawlik and Augsten (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pawlik and Augsten (2015)</a></cite>, and (2) Jaccard similarity, calculated as the intersection-over-union of component names in the scripts, to measure the overlap in pure semantic content. This experiment was limited to the spell DSL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Automated Qualitative Assessment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because large-scale human evaluation was infeasible, we used an automated LLM judge based on <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPT 4.1</span> to provide qualitative ratings at scale, justifying its use with a rigorous validation process. For all valid outputs, the judge assigned 1-5 Likert scores for the 4 criteria described below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(i) Creative Alignment:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This scale assesses how well the generated output captured the user’s core creative intent, with an emphasis on theme.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(ii) Instruction Following:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This scale measures how accurately the output adhered to specific, direct commands or constraints in player requests.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(iii) Emergence:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures whether the output surpassed the user’s request in a positive and unexpected way, such as environmental interaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(iv) Structural Coherence:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluates adherence to the DSL’s logical rules. This was designed to provide a more comprehensive measure after basic syntactic validity was found to be very high.

<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:12.0pt;">
The LLM judge (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPT 4.1</span>) was instructed to produce a textual evaluation of each script before assigning a numerical score, a form of Chain-of-Thought prompting used to improve reliability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite>. To protect against a common leniency bias in LLM judges, a large model and careful prompting were used in accordance with established guidelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
The prompt for the LLM judge is available in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Judge Model Prompt ‣ Appendix A Abridged Prompts ‣ Real-Time World Crafting: Generating Structured Game Behaviors from Natural Language with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
